PowerVLC: drop the libdispatch dependency from the Darwin MMC backend

src/file/mmc_device_darwin.c drives its DiskArbitration session from a serial
dispatch queue and synchronises with a dispatch semaphore. That needs:

  - DASessionSetDispatchQueue  -> Mac OS X 10.7
  - dispatch_semaphore_*, dispatch_time, dispatch_sync, dispatch_release -> 10.6
  - a block (^{ ... }) for dispatch_sync -> clang only

PowerVLC's 64-bit Intel slice is built with a 10.5 deployment target and the
contribs are compiled with -Werror=partial-availability, so libaacs simply did
not build for it -- which meant no AACS, i.e. no retail Blu-ray, on that slice.

Replaced with facilities that all predate 10.4:

  - a dedicated thread running a CFRunLoop, with the session attached through
    DASessionScheduleWithRunLoop(); the session is scheduled and the approval
    callback registered on that same thread, so callbacks stay serialised
    exactly as the serial queue guaranteed;
  - a counting semaphore built from a pthread mutex + condition variable,
    with pthread_cond_timedwait() for the 20 second re-appear timeout;
  - the dispatch_sync() block replaced by taking the callback mutex around the
    disk_state check. iokit_da_destroy() is deliberately called after dropping
    that mutex: it joins the event thread, which would deadlock against a
    callback blocked on the mutex.

Note this only makes libaacs buildable where the SDK has a Blu-ray device
class at all. The PowerPC and Intel-32 slices build against the 10.4u/10.5
SDKs, and neither ships IOKit/storage/IOBDMediaBSDClient.h, so libaacs stays
out of those (see contrib/src/aacs/rules.mak).

